-- title:
--   Closedness of the trivial-fibre locus for a rigidified Pic⁰-family
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a Noetherian commutative ring, let $C$ be a scheme and let $c : C \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and let $\varepsilon$ be a section of $c$, that is, a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} R \to C$ whose composite with $c$ is the identity. Assume that $c$ and $\varepsilon$ admit finite map data of arbitrarily large degree: for every $m_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ there is a datum $\mathfrak{F}$ of type `SmoothProperCurve.FiniteMapData c ε` with $m_0 \le \mathfrak{F}.m$, such a datum consisting of two affine opens $U, V$ covering $C$ with $U$ exactly the complement of the image of $\varepsilon$, sections $f \in \Gamma(C,U)$ and $g \in \Gamma(C,V)$ whose basic opens both equal $U \cap V$ and whose restrictions to $U \cap V$ multiply to $1$, finiteness of $\Gamma(C,U)$ over $R[X]$ via $f$ and of $\Gamma(C,V)$ over $R[X]$ via $g$, and the requirement that for every local $R$-algebra $S$ and every $s \in S$ the quotient $S \otimes_R \Gamma(C,U)$ by the ideal generated by $1 \otimes f - s \otimes 1$ is finite free of rank $\mathfrak{F}.m$ over $S$. Let further $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ be locally of finite type and let $L$ be a rigidified line bundle for $c$, $\varepsilon$ over $t$: a module $L.L$ on the fibre product $C \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} T$ which is locally isomorphic to the unit (invertible), together with a trivialisation of its pullback along the rigidifying section $T \to C \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} T$ determined by $\varepsilon$. Assume $L$ satisfies `FibrewiseAlgEquivZero`: for every algebraically closed field $k$ and every $s : \operatorname{Spec} k \to T$, the pullback of $L.L$ to the fibre $(C \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} T) \times_T \operatorname{Spec} k$ is algebraically equivalent to zero over the structure morphism of that fibre, in the sense that there are a locally of finite type, geometrically integral $h : T' \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, an invertible module $M$ on the fibre $\times_{\operatorname{Spec} k} T'$ and two $k$-points $t_0, t_1$ of $T'$ such that the restriction of $M$ at $t_0$ is isomorphic to the unit and its restriction at $t_1$ is isomorphic to the given bundle. The conclusion is that the set of points $x \in T$ for which there exist a field $k$ and a morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec} k \to T$ sending the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} k$ to $x$ and such that the fibre module `fibreModule c t s L.L`, the pullback of $L.L$ along the first projection of $(C \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} T) \times_T \operatorname{Spec} k$, is isomorphic to the monoidal unit of the modules on that fibre, is a closed subset of $T$.
--
--   This is the scheme-free form of the statement that the unit section of the Jacobian of a relative smooth proper curve is a closed immersion: the locus in the base over which a family in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ becomes trivial on some field-valued fibre is closed. It is used by [`AlgebraicGeometry.RelPicard.isSeparated_of_representsRelSubPic_algEquivZeroCut_of_finiteMapData`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.RelPicard.isSeparated_of_representsRelSubPic_algEquivZeroCut_of_finiteMapData) to deduce that a scheme representing the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ functor cut out by algebraic equivalence to zero is separated.
